course: everyday
lesson: 475
title: The row over a game
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Sam and Jake are playing Mario Kart. Jake says Sam cheated with the shortcut; Sam says the shortcut is part of the game. Controllers hit the sofa. «You're such a sore loser!» «You're such a cheat!» Jake storms home. Two days of silence. Then Jake turns up at the door with a packet of Monster Munch: «Rematch? No shortcuts?» Sam is so relieved he laughs. Most rows over games are about winning, not about the friend.
explain: Some game rows hide another feeling. Lily deletes Maddie's Roblox tower «by accident». Maddie says, «You did it on purpose, you're well jel» («jel» is short for jealous). Lily admits she was annoyed Maddie's tower was better, and helps rebuild it, bigger. Owning up to the real feeling fixes it faster than «it was an accident».
explain: Rows get worse with hands and with phones. Connor pushes Kieran off the trampoline in a row over turns, and Kieran bangs his elbow; Connor is sent home and apologises next day, red-faced, his mum behind him. Raj rage-quits FIFA (leaves the game in a temper) when he's losing 5–0, and Connor messages «L + ratio», a meme way of saying «you lost and nobody's on your side». Raj blocks him. They only make up at school when Connor admits the message was «a bit much». At Christmas Mia's cousin flips the Monopoly board across the room, so the aunties ban Monopoly at Christmas for good. Chloe switches the dance game off mid-song when she's losing to Aisha, then later texts: «sorry, I was being a sore loser». And when Jake blames Sam for a missed goal, the coach says, «Win as a team, lose as a team.»
explain: Some rows are tiny. Jake and Kieran each swear they touched the lamppost first, and don't speak for a week, until Kieran's little sister says, «You both touched it at the same time, you eejits.» Lily and Maddie shout about whether tig-backs (tagging straight back the person who tagged you) are allowed, while the other kids wander off to play football. Next time they write the rules down first. And when Mia's friend says «you're terrible at this game, honestly», Mia goes quiet all day, then says, «It didn't feel like a joke.»
fact: Arguments over games usually come from wanting to win, not from hating the friend.
fact: Cooling off first and coming back with something small, like a snack and a «rematch?», often mends a game row.
fact: Saying the real feeling, such as «I was jealous», mends a row faster than pretending it was an accident.
fact: Pushing someone during a row can really hurt them; walk away when you are angry.
fact: A sore loser gets upset or angry about losing; everybody loses sometimes.
fact: Agreeing the rules before a game starts leaves less to fight about.
fact: Teasing someone after beating them online can turn a game into a real fall-out.
fact: Blaming a teammate for a loss splits the team; teams win and lose together.
fact: Very small rows can often be ended with a laugh.
fact: If a friend's «joke» hurts, it is fair to tell them; a good friend listens.
fact: «Jel» means jealous, «rage-quit» means leaving a game in a temper, and «tig» (or «tag») is the chasing game.
exercise: Why did Sam and Jake row over Mario Kart? | Jake said the shortcut was cheating | Sam broke Jake's new controller by throwing it | Jake ate all the snacks | Sam wouldn't let Jake play
exercise: How did Jake end the row? | He came back with Monster Munch and asked for a rematch | He sent his mum round to say sorry to Sam's mum for him | He bought a new game | He never came back
exercise: What does «jel» mean? | Jealous | Jelly | Gentle | Joking around
exercise: What fixed the Roblox row between Lily and Maddie? | Lily admitted she was jealous and helped rebuild | Maddie deleted Lily's tower too, so they were even | They both quit Roblox | A teacher deleted both towers
exercise: Connor pushed Kieran off the trampoline. What happened? | Kieran hurt his elbow and Connor was sent home | They laughed about it and just kept on jumping | Kieran pushed Connor off too | Nobody noticed
exercise: Mia's cousin flipped the Monopoly board. What did the aunties do? | Banned Monopoly at Christmas | Bought a brand-new board for them | Made the cousin win next time | Sent everyone to bed early instead
exercise: Chloe turned the dance game off when losing. How did she fix it? | Texted Aisha «sorry, I was being a sore loser» | Told Aisha the game was broken and needed fixing | Never played with Aisha again | Bought Aisha a new game
exercise: Jake blamed Sam for a missed goal. What did the coach say? | Win as a team, lose as a team | Sam should be dropped for the next match | Jake should be captain | Missing is never allowed
exercise: How did the lamppost row end? | Kieran's sister said they touched it together and they laughed | A teacher came out and measured who was closest to it | They raced again ten times | They stayed enemies forever
exercise: What did Connor send Raj after Raj rage-quit? | A teasing «L + ratio» message | A sorry message for winning | A gift in the game | Nothing at all
exercise: Why did Raj block Connor? | Connor teased him | Connor cheated in the game | Connor stole his password | Raj's mum told him to block him
exercise: Lily and Maddie argued about tig-backs. What did they agree for next time? | Write the rules down before starting | Let the teacher on duty always decide the rules | Never play tig again | Play only on their own
exercise: What happened to the other kids while Lily and Maddie argued? | They went and played football | They joined the shouting | They waited quietly for hours | They told the head teacher
exercise: Mia's friend said she was terrible at the game. What did Mia finally say? | «It didn't feel like a joke» | «You're terrible too» | «I'm never playing again» | «I know, I'm the worst»
exercise: What are most rows over games really about? | Wanting to win | Hating the other person | Being hungry | Money
test: «he took the shortcut, that's cheating, I'm never speaking to him again». Is it likely to last forever? | No, game rows usually blow over after cooling off | Yes, game rows always end friendships for good | Yes, shortcuts are always cheating | No, but only if he pays
test: Lily: «I deleted it by accident, honest». It wasn't an accident. What would fix it fastest? | Owning up to feeling jealous | Sticking to the accident story | Deleting her own tower too, to be fair | Blaming the internet connection
test: «He took my turn so I'll just shove him off». What would probably happen? | Someone could get hurt | He'd say sorry and give you the turn back | Everyone would agree you were right | Nothing, trampolines are soft
test: «L + ratio, get rekt» after winning 5–0. Why can that turn into a real fall-out? | Teasing a loser hurts more than losing | It's a nice way to say well played, everyone knows | Raj likes being teased | It can't, it's just words
test: «this game is trash» says Chloe, switching off mid-song while losing. What's going on? | She's annoyed with losing, not with the game | The game really is broken and keeps skipping songs | Aisha is cheating | The music is too loud
test: Coach hears Jake: «we lost cos Sam missed, he's a muppet». What does a good team say? | We win and lose together | Sam should never play again | Only Sam is to blame for it | Only Jake is to blame for it
test: «I touched it first!» «No, I did!» They don't talk for a week. What does this show? | Some rows are tiny and a laugh can end them | Lampposts are dangerous | They should have a proper fight to settle it | They'll be enemies forever
test: «tig-backs are allowed, everyone knows that, innit». What's the best way to stop this kind of row? | Agree the rules before you start | The loudest person decides | Stop playing games forever | Let whoever is it make up rules
test: In a cartoon, two pals smash controllers and never speak again. What happens more often in real life? | They cool off and play again | They never speak to each other again | They buy new friends | They move house
test: «it was just a joke, why u so salty??» («salty» means upset or bitter.) Mia was hurt. Who should listen? | The friend who joked | Nobody, jokes never hurt | Only Mia's teacher at school | Only Mia's mum, nobody else
test: Monopoly board flipped across the room. What rule could stop it next time? | Agree first: no flipping, no sulking | Only play with whoever won last time | Glue the board down | Never let cousins play
exam: Identify the principal cause, as established in the lesson, of disputes arising between friends during games. | The desire to win rather than enmity towards the friend | A deep and lasting hatred between the players | Defects in the equipment employed | The intervention of parents
exam: Which of the following measures is most likely to prevent disputes concerning the rules of a game? | Agreement upon the rules prior to commencement | The adoption of new rules during play as needs arise | Deference to the loudest participant | The abandonment of all games
exam: Ascertain the most effective means, as established in the lesson, of reconciliation following a quarrel in which one party has falsely claimed an accident. | A candid acknowledgement of the true feeling, such as jealousy | Persistence in the account of an accident | Retaliation in kind against the other party | The avoidance of the other party indefinitely
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a remark presented as a jest which causes distress. | It is reasonable to inform the speaker that it caused hurt | The distressed party is necessarily at fault for lacking humour | A jest can never occasion distress | Such remarks ought to be repeated more loudly
